Important command of linux administration

To list items in a directory - ls

Create directory -- mkdir

To list the system processes running - top / htop

Manages system services and the systemd init system. Use it to start, stop, enable, disable, or check the status of services (e.g., systemctl status nginx). - systemctl

Reports file system disk space usage. Administrative flags like df -h show readable breakdowns of free and used space on mounted drives. - df

Estimates file space usage. Use du -sh * to quickly find out which specific files or directories are hogging storage space - du

Configures and displays network interfaces, routing, and tunnels. It replaces the deprecated ifconfig command (e.g., ip a to show IP addresses) - ip

Modifies file permissions and ownership. Essential for securing files, adjusting read/write access, and fixing application permission errors - chmod /chown
